  "banner_title": "Trump threatens to DOUBLE Mexico tariffs over new migrant caravans — Mexico-exposed supply chains on watch",
  "banner_title_zh": "特朗普威胁对新移民大篷车加倍征收墨西哥关税——墨西哥相关供应链承压",
  "summary": "President Trump has explicitly threatened to immediately double tariffs on Mexico if it does not stop new migrant caravans, while doubling down on a closed-border stance after Democrats took the House. The tariff threat is the market-relevant transmission channel, with concentrated exposure in autos, Mexican manufacturing, and cross-border agriculture.",
  "summary_zh": "特朗普明确威胁，若墨西哥不阻止新移民大篷车，将立即加倍对墨西哥关税；同时民主党掌控众议院后，他继续强化关闭边境立场。关税威胁是市场相关传导渠道，汽车、墨西哥制造业和跨境农业敞口集中。",
  "analysis": "The announcement is primarily political messaging, but it contains a concrete, market-moving policy threat embedded in the President's quoted statement: that if Mexico does not stop the caravans, 'the Tariffs on Mexico will be IMMEDIATELY DOUBLED.' This is not a signed action, but it escalates the trade-policy risk premium on any supply chain with Mexican exposure, especially given the existing USMCA framework and prior tariff history.\n\nThe transmission mechanism runs through input costs and margins for US companies sourcing from Mexico — autos and auto parts are the most obvious, followed by electronics, appliances, avocados and other produce, and industrial goods. A doubling of tariffs on Mexico would function as a supply-side shock, raising costs and potentially forcing reconfiguration of North American supply chains that have deepened since USMCA.\n\nThe key uncertainty is execution versus rhetoric. The White House has used tariff threats as leverage before, and this reads as a pressure tactic tied to immigration enforcement rather than a formal trade action. However, the explicit, specific language — 'IMMEDIATELY DOUBLED' — and the political backdrop (a new Democratic House vowing to dismantle ICE/DHS) raise the odds that immigration-trade linkage becomes an active policy tool again.",
